● Your instinct is pointing at something real, and I want to separate it into the two questions tangled together: was OpEnvelope the right abstraction to build with (I'd say yes), and is it the right abstraction to state the final result with (I'd say no — and the fix is a layer on top, not a rewrite).

  What OpEnvelope actually is, stated plainly. It's a sum type whose constructors bundle data (rows, bus entries) together with evidence (validator instances, row facts, route facts, promise bundles). So ∀ env : OpEnvelope, … → env.exec_eq is literally "for every package of data-plus-proofs, compliance holds." That shape has two structural
  consequences you've been living with for months. First, every constructor field is a potential hiding place for an assumption — which is why you needed the entire anti-laundering apparatus, the caller-burden ledger's 1,087 lines, the hypothesis-count gate, the constructibility witnesses. Those aren't incidental bureaucracy; they're the
  carrying cost of an abstraction that makes trust ambient and diffuse rather than concentrated in one visible place. Second, the statement isn't self-interpreting: nobody can read the global theorem and know what's assumed without reading 63 constructors plus a ledger. That's the sharpness deficit you're feeling.

  Why it was still the right scaffolding. The honest counterfactual isn't "the same result, stated better." The textbook-sharp alternative — quantify over the raw committed trace, assume only ConstraintsHold ∧ ChannelsBalanced ∧ BootOK, conclude a step-simulation against Sail — is a monolithic statement. You can't land it opcode by opcode,
  you can't carry an undischarged promise visibly while you work on the next family, and you can't measure progress with a ledger, because there's one giant simulation invariant instead of 63 inspectable burdens. The envelope is what let this project move incrementally and let the gates mechanically freeze the trust surface at every step.
  The OUTPUT-EQ retirement, the promise-discharge campaign, the load closures — all of that worked because the obligations were reified as fields you could see, count, and remove one at a time.

  Why it's the wrong final interface — and the cheap path to the right one. The sharp statement you want is reachable as a composition, and it's exactly what #61 already asks for:

  theorem zisk_riscv_compliant_trace
      (trace : CommittedTrace) (program : Program n)
      (h_constraints : ConstraintsHold trace)      -- from extraction, the thing the proof system enforces
      (h_balance : ChannelsBalanced trace)
      (h_boot : InitialStateOK trace program)       -- the genuine public-input premises
      : StepwiseSailCorrespondence trace program

  with AcceptedTrace → OpEnvelope (the #61 construction theorem) as the internal bridge and the current global theorem consumed inside. At that point OpEnvelope vanishes from the public signature, the 1,087 binder lines collapse into one constraint-satisfaction predicate that is derived rather than assumed per-opcode, and the
  constructibility worry inverts from "can envelopes be built?" into a theorem that builds them. You get the sharper statement without abandoning anything — the per-opcode canonical theorems remain exactly the lemmas any architecture would have needed.

  The diagnostic that answers "too broad?" definitively. Classify every envelope field into three buckets: (a) derivable from constraint satisfaction plus channel balance — these get discharged by the construction theorem; (b) genuine top-level premises — boot state, program binding, the Rust-bridge and (until #76) memory-timeline facts —
  these belong explicitly in the final trace-level signature; (c) neither — not derivable from what the proof system enforces, and not a defensible public premise. Bucket (c) is the test: if it's empty, OpEnvelope was a fine waypoint and only the export layer is missing. If things land in (c), those specific fields are where the
  abstraction genuinely baked in assumptions a different design would have surfaced earlier — and they get targeted fixes, not a rewrite. The caller-burden ledger's category labels (validator | row | bridge | bus_shape | byte_chain | …) already do half of this classification for you, and #74's Phase 0 — enumerating everything the
  constructors demand for one opcode — is the other half done concretely. The memory timeline is the instructive precedent: it sat in bucket (c) disguised as a constructor-carried promise until #63/#65 forced it into bucket (b) as a named top-level hypothesis, and #76 is the plan to move it to (a). The architecture question is whether
  anything else like it is still hiding.

  So my recommendation, concretely: don't rearchitect, and don't treat the worry as resolved either. Run the bucket classification as the first deliverable of the #61 program (it falls out of #74's Phase 0 nearly for free), put the trace-level export theorem at the top of #61's success criteria as the public statement, and judge OpEnvelope
  on the outcome — if bucket (c) is empty, it was scaffolding that did its job and the final result is exactly as sharp as the from-scratch design would have been; if not, you'll know precisely which fields to fix and the blast radius of each. Want me to fold this framing into the #61 refresh comment — recast its success criteria around
  the trace-level export theorem and the three-bucket audit?

