● The adversarial pass came back SOUND-AND-MINIMAL — ready to implement, having
  independently re-derived the soundness against the real Clean source (no
  double-count, no dropped interaction, no hidden sorry/axiom). As promised, here's
  the design before I touch Clean.

  The insight (why it's safe)

  reqs_disjoint_finished is conservative, not load-bearing. It exists to discharge
  an OrderedChannel obligation that has two sufficient disjuncts:
  - "the VM tables add no requirements on this finished channel" (what addVm
  currently demands — the lookup case), or
  - "the VM tables add no guarantees on this finished channel" (the provider case).

  A pure provider like our Mem rows — which only push MemBus, never pull it —
  satisfies the second. The finished channel's balance is taken globally over all
  tables, so the provider's push is counted exactly once either way; nothing is
  dropped or double-counted. The only thing that changes is how we prove the VM
  tables meet MemBus's guarantee obligation, and for a pure provider that's vacuous
  (it guarantees nothing on MemBus).

  The change (minimal, idiomatic, additive)

  Replace the precondition with a disjunction, in a new sibling builder — leaving
  the existing addVm and its only caller (FibonacciWithChannels) untouched:

  -- for each finished channel: (no requirements) OR (no guarantees)
  (∀ channel ∈ finished,
    (channel ∉ vm.verifier.channelsWithRequirements ∧
      ∀ t ∈ vm.tables, channel ∉ t.circuit.channelsWithRequirements) ∨
    (channel ∉ vm.verifier.channelsWithGuarantees ∧
      ∀ t ∈ vm.tables, channel ∉ t.circuit.channelsWithGuarantees))

  Footprint — Clean/Air/Vm.lean only: +1 core theorem (the existing
  addVm_soundVmChannel_of_soundChannels body verbatim except a 3-spot fork: the
  precondition, the per-channel reshape, and the two consumer sites) + 1 builder
  addVmProvidingFinished + 4 rfl projection lemmas. OrderedChannel.lean untouched;
  existing addVm untouched. No sorry/axiom. ZisK then discharges MemBus's disjunct
  via the provider branch (Or.inr — Mem's MemBus-guarantees list is empty), closable
  by the simp [circuit_norm] default.

  One honest caveat (the next wall, not a defect here)

  This patch unblocks the table-assembly layer. It does not by itself finish the
  migration: addVmProvidingFinished returns a SoundVmEnsemble, which forces
  switching the ensemble's closure from SoundEnsemble.toFormal (Spec := True) to
  SoundVmEnsemble.toFormal (Spec := ∃ data, VerifierSpec), surfacing the seam
  verifier's VerifierSpec/verifier_requirements obligations. That's the migration's
  known re-prove territory — expected, but real, and separate from this Clean
  change.

  Riskiest implementation step (so I tackle it first)

  Getting the reqs_disjoint reshape under the fork right — scoping it per-channel so
  the lookup branch still feeds guarantees_of_requirements_append the all-tables
  form it expects. Everything else is the original proof body verbatim or the
  vacuous provider branch.

