Got the exact signature — and it lets me give you a precise answer. Yes, there is a composite theorem, and it is proven. Here it is:

theorem zisk_riscv_compliant_program_bus
    (env : OpEnvelope state m r_main)                                  -- the per-op proof package
    (h_bridge : env.aeneasBridgeTrust)                                 -- decode faithfulness
    (h_memory_construction : env.memoryTimelineConstructionEvidence)   -- memory timeline
    (h_known_bugs : Defects.NoKnownDefect env) :                       -- defect exclusion
    env.exec_eq                                                        -- ← end-to-end compliance

env.exec_eq is the composite statement: for the program execution, the circuit's decode-then-execute behavior matches the Sail spec, step by step. It chains the pieces — decode (h_bridge) + per-op execution (inside env) + memory (h_memory_construction) → compliance. So it's not three disconnected islands; the link exists and typechecks.

But your underlying instinct is right about how it's linked — it's conditional on four supplied inputs, and two of them are exactly the "not-yet-composed-from-raw-data" parts:

1. env : OpEnvelope is supplied, not derived. This is the proof-bearing package that carries the 63 per-op equivalences plus their construction facts (provider/row evidence, promises). The per-op equivalences themselves are proven in-build (the 63 equiv_<OP>), so this input is real proof, not a hole — but the theorem assumes the envelope exists; whether every accepted trace can produce one from raw trace data is #61, and that's exactly what the construction_<op>_sound sweep (47/63) is closing. So the per-op execution side is soundly linked, but the "from raw trace" composition is in progress.
2. h_bridge : aeneasBridgeTrust is the loose link. This is where the decoder facts (Piece 1) plug in — but only as a hypothesis backed by the side workspace + manifest, not an in-build proof term (#111). So the decoder→composite link is the weak one.
3. h_memory_construction = #76 (open). h_known_bugs = the documented defect exclusion.

So the honest one-paragraph version: the composite is proven, but conditionally. The execution half is soundly in-build (the per-op proofs are real and the composite consumes them); the decode half is loosely coupled (the spot your instinct flagged); and the whole thing currently assumes the OpEnvelope rather than building it from raw trace data. The endgame discharges exactly these conditions: P4→P5 / #61 makes the OpEnvelope trace-derivable (so it's no longer a supplied input), #76 discharges the memory hypothesis, #111 would turn the loose decode link into a real proof term. After all three, the composite holds with its remaining trust being only the documented external pieces (the Sail spec itself, the Charon/Aeneas translation, and the recorded defects) — which is the irreducible floor.

So: not "we haven't linked it" — we have, and it's proven. The accurate gap is that the composite is conditional, and the conditions (envelope-from-trace, sound decode link, memory timeline) are precisely the threads in flight.

