❯ I missed some a/b/c list you said is blocking, where is that?

That's the #111 route decision — how to discharge aeneasBridgeTrust (the decode bridge), given the spike found the sound in-build discharge is a NO-GO (GATE 1: the numBits-opacity + @[irreducible] wall blocks plain decide/simp; GATE 2: the only route that works, native_decide, is gate-forbidden). The three options:

- (a) Large sound effort — prove the decode pins via Aeneas progress/scalar_tac symbolic execution of the ~150-function transpile, per opcode, with a System.Platform.numBits 32/64 case split. Principled, 0 new trust axioms, but a genuine multi-week workstream (not the cheap per-opcode decide the import-GO had hoped for).
- (b) native_decide trust-policy — extend the gate's axiom allowlist to permit ofReduceBool/trustCompiler for the aeneas path, then use the native_decide discharge (which does work). Cheap, but it moves native-compiler trust into the gated build — directly against the project's #75 effort to eliminate native_decide. I advised against.
- (c) Keep the loose coupling — status quo (cross-workspace lean-check proofs + manifest gate + CI); the a2fcf1923d / Charon-0.1.184 downgrade recipe is banked in #111 for whenever (a) is done. I've been holding here.

Where it's written down: issue #111 (the "Routes forward" section of my comment — https://github.com/eth-act/zisk-fv/issues/111), and the #111 line in STATUS.md / PLAN_ENDGAME.md.

My recommendation: (c) now, (a) eventually, avoid (b). It only "blocks" in the sense that it was clause (2) of the /goal you just cleared — it's not blocking the #61/P5 work at all. So it's independent of the strictly-stronger-theorem question; you can decide it whenever, or leave it at (c).


