The research is back, and it's an important answer. Bottom line: there is no silently untracked give-up — but the scale and framing of what's assumed is bigger than the "0-axiom" headline suggests, and that's the real story.

The honest answer to "what did we give up on"

Good news first: every execution-necessary constraint the FV drops maps to an existing issue (#114, #100, #76/#115, #61/#74) or a named residual hypothesis tracked by the caller-burden ledger. No hidden class-(C) hole. The two residuals you're already discharging (div0/overflow, na=MSB) are a small slice of a much larger, already-enumerated set.

The real "give-up" is architectural — and under-emphasized: the gated theorem zisk_riscv_compliant_program_bus does not derive the cross-AIR relationships from a composed, globally-balanced ensemble. It assumes them per-arm — each opcode takes its provider table, a row, a matches_entry bus equality, and lane-matches as hypotheses. Crucially, ZisK's actual soundness mechanism — the global zero-sum gsum·global_den + global_num === 0 (std_sum.pil:745), the check that makes every bus/lookup/permutation argument mean anything — has no enforced FV analog. The FV's stand-in (witness.BalancedChannels) is itself a hypothesis, never derived from committed columns. --skip-unsupported's real effect isn't the four bailed operand kinds — it's that every lookup/permutation/range/global-sum compiles to stage-2 accumulator columns the extractor drops entirely, leaving each as a hint or nothing.

So what we "gave up on" (deferred) is: deriving the bus/lookup/range/balance relationships from circuit data instead of assuming them. That's sound but per-arm-conditional and incomplete — and it's the precise scope of "the extracted circuit matches Sail."

Why it's incompleteness, not unsoundness

Dropping a constraint makes Valid_<AIR> weaker (admits more traces) → the theorem becomes a stronger claim → it surfaces as residual hypotheses, never vacuity. A trace ZisK rejects (unbalanced bus, forged provider row, non-increasing memory step) can satisfy the per-arm hypotheses — but the theorem doesn't falsely call it compliant; it just hasn't been shown those hypotheses are dischargeable from real circuit data.

Three concrete things worth surfacing

1. Whole range/table AIRs are absent from the FV — SpecifiedRanges (#19), VirtualTable0/1 (#20/21), ArithRangeTable: zero occurrences in ZiskFv/; range checks are hand-rebuilt as row-local StaticTables, not composed (#114 cat C).
2. One specific drop most likely overlooked: Arith constraints 47, 48 (m32·bus_a1 === 0, m32·bus_b1 === 0, arith.pil:264-265) — the W-mode operand high-word zeroing that MULW/DIVW/REMW correctness rests on. It's --only-dropped with no stub, and it's neither the div block nor na=MSB — so it's exactly the thing that falls through when "#114 cat A" is mentally equated with "the div fix."
3. The framing gap itself: nothing states, as a headline auditor-facing caveat, "the global balance check is assumed, not verified."

Connection to this session's work

The strong export I built (zisk_compliant_of_accepted_trace_strong) is actually a step against this — it derives the per-arm matches from trace.balanced (one trace-level balance assumption instead of 63 per-arm matches_entry ones). But trace.balanced is still an input, not derived from the committed columns + the gsum zero-sum. Closing that — deriving balance from the circuit's actual global-sum constraint — is the heart of #74.

Suggested follow-ups (your call): (a) a finer-grained #114 sub-item for Arith 47/48 (W-mode operand zeroing), and (b) a dedicated issue stating the global-balance-assumed-not-derived scope plainly (the single most important thing for an auditor; currently implied across #61/#74/#100/#115 but never headlined). Want me to file those two?


