Reading the sizes

- The two giants are #114 and #74 — and they're the same mega-goal from two sides: #114 extracts the bus/lookup/range/global-zero-sum constraints the real circuit enforces (broadest — spans every AIR), and #74 composes them into an end-to-end derivation (deepest — the global-balance/gsum derivation the research flagged as the dominant assumption). Close both and the "0-axiom" claim stops being per-arm-conditional.
- The deep residuals (#100, #111, #115) are L-sized — each is a genuine mechanism (cross-row execution bus, Aeneas-on-4.28, whole-trace memory induction). #111 is gated by a toolchain blocker, so its effort is high even though its scope is narrow.
- The bounded ones (#101, #119) are S–M — a specific lemma and an OpEnvelope refactor; finishable in one focused sitting each.
- Hardening (#75/#77/#78) is M and independent — doesn't block the soundness story; quality/auditability wins.
- Cleanup is essentially done — #116/#117 close on #120; only #118 (the broader sweep, M+) remains, and it's gated on #117's tool fix + a better safety net.

Net shape: it's two big trunks (derive #61 ⟸ extract #114) joined at the root by the cross-link, a handful of deep-but-narrow residuals hanging off #61, and three small independent hardening tracks. The realistic "critical path to a non-conditional global theorem" runs #114 → #74, with #100/#111/#115 feeding #74 in parallel.



