● Grounded answer — and it sharpens the picture usefully. The key realization: there's one "decode-binding" axis here, and #159 closed the ZisK half of it. Almost
  everything else in root_soundness is a different axis that #111 never touched.

  Unblocked now (by the #159 stack)

  The op-identity decode columns are now derived from the committed raw program, not assumed:
  op, is_external_op, m32, set_pc, store_pc, jmp_offset1/2, ind_width, flags — i.e. #111's pins are load-bearing. The rowDecodes premise is no longer a caller
  assumption.
  - #74 (instantiate root_soundness on a real trace) — its decode-driven rowData was blocked on #61/#159; the decode witness is now constructible. (Still needs the other
  premises built, but the decode blocker is lifted.)
  - Advances #61 (the umbrella rowData gap) on its decode child.

  Still blocked by #172

  The Sail-instruction + operand decoding grounded in the raw word — i.e. the full "root_soundness is about the circuit executing the committed program." Right now
  operandsGrounded = false: the Sail conclusion instruction.RTYPE (c.r2, c.r1, c.rd, …) takes its operands from the caller's ziskStep, not from rawProgram. #172 would
  close that, and it's blocked by the fundamental noncomputable Sail decoder — plus it needs #174 (de-native_decide the Sail encode bridge) for kernel-soundness.
  - Important: #172 is not blocked by #111. It's a different decoder (the Sail spec's encdec_backwards), so #159 doesn't help it — which is exactly why it's a separate,
  hard issue rather than a punt of #170's work.

  Never about #111 — independent axes (neither unblocked by #159 nor blocked by #172)

  This is the bulk of the remaining trust, and it's the honest counterweight to "is #172 the last thing":
  - Operand values (inputsAgree) — the cross-world register/PC/memory value bridge. The fundamental assumption; orthogonal to decoding.
  - Effect placement (#141) — dest-reg / store-address (next-PC was #100). A sibling "derive from Main AIR" axis #159 didn't touch.
  - Memory timeline (#76) — loads/stores.
  - Signed-M defects — the real codygunton/zisk#5 bug, defect-gated (#151 → #169).
  - Boot/profile, aeneasBridgeTrust, channel-balance, completeness (#154).
  - The non-ROM operand witnesses still carried in RawRowDecode (shift amounts, signed-load BinaryExtension, M-ext bits) — operand-side, not the op-decode #159 grounded.

  Punchline: #159 fully closed one axis (which opcode + flags, grounded in the committed binary). #172 is the matching Sail-side twin of that axis — real remaining work,
  genuinely blocked at a tooling wall, and complementary to the half that's now done. It is not the only thing left, and not the critical-path blocker: the heavier
  remaining trust (operand values, placement, memory, the signed-M bug) is independent of both #111 and #172. So #172 is honestly-carved, not a hole dug under #170.

